 "brief": "Analytic dilogarithm identities — We introduce dilogarithm identities through a beta integral-based technique that we apply to provide analytic proofs of previously conjectured dilogarithm relations",
 "extended": "**Analytic dilogarithm identities**\n\n*Moreover, single-term dilogarithm evaluations are introduced and derived*\n\n40 pp, 64 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** We introduce dilogarithm identities through a beta integral-based technique that we apply to provide analytic proofs of previously conjectured dilogarithm relations, solving open problems given by both Bytsko and Campbell, and that we further apply to construct and prove new ladder relations with quartic and sextic bases. We also apply our method to introduce and prove two-term Li 2-relations and ladder-like identities with arguments in algebraic number fields such asQ( √ 2), Q( √ 3), Q( √ 5), and Q(√−7). Moreover, single-term dilogarithm evaluations are introduced and derived.\n\n**Outline.** Abstract; 1 Introduction; 2 (7ζ(3) + (3− 2G)π− 12); 1.1 Preliminaries; 2 Li(x) + 2 Li(−x) = Li(x2), (5); 2 Li2(x)− 1; 2 Li2(−x) = 1; 2 Sextic integrals and dilogarithm identities; 2 (1−y)2n+1, (15); 2 Li2; 8 Li2; 3 Li2; 3 Applications; 3.1 A full solution to a problem due to Sun; 3.2 On an identity attributed to Lima; 3.3 Zagier’s four-term identity; 27 Li2; 3.4 Identities related of an evaluation for th\n\n**Extracted claims (proposals, not verified).**\n- Theorem 2: The closed-form evaluation\n- Theorem 1: is used. Consequently, we obtain the equality of\n- Theorem 3: For complex u, letL(u) denote the three-term dilogarithm combination\n- Theorem 4: The three-term dilogarithm combination\n- Theorem 6: For the unique rooth in the interval (0, 1) ofx6−x5−x4−6x3−x2−x+1 = 0,\n- Theorem 7: Let h denote the unique root in the interval (0, 1) of x6− 3x5 + 5x4− 8x3 +",
